"1.7\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:0:{}}}s:7:\"plugins\";O:8:\"stdClass\":8:{s:19:\"akismet/akismet.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:5:\"5.7.2\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:0:{}}s:36:\"contact-form-7/wp-contact-form-7.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:5:\"6.2.2\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:3:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":7:{s:2:\"id\";s:36:\"6b0a5d1f-84fb-425f-8d37-ddde5703937c\";s:5:\"title\";s:93:\"Contact Form 7 6.2 - 6.2.3 - Unauthenticated Arbitrary File Upload via Invalid UTF-8 Filename\";s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"6.2.4\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";s:3:\"6.2\";s:17:\"affected_versions\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"6.2.4\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";s:3:\"6.2\";}}s:11:\"description\";s:691:\"The plugin does not validate the type or extension of files uploaded through a form file field, and a flaw in its temporary-file cleanup leaves the uploaded file on disk, allowing unauthenticated users to upload arbitrary files to the site. On a stock Debian or Ubuntu Apache configuration, which lists directory contents and maps .phar to the PHP handler, the stored file can be located and executed, leading to Remote Code Execution. On other configurations, including nginx and Apache with .htaccess overrides enabled, the issue is limited to an arbitrary file written into the uploads directory, which may still be served as active content such as HTML or SVG from the site\'s own origin.\";s:8:\"severity\";i:4;}i:1;O:8:\"stdClass\":7:{s:2:\"id\";s:36:\"a981358b-6736-4d46-9352-b57a11dbcb54\";s:5:\"title\";s:87:\"Contact Form 7 < 6.2.5 - Editor+ Sensitive User Data Disclosure via Form Default Values\";s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"6.2.5\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;s:17:\"affected_versions\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"6.2.5\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;}}s:11:\"description\";s:313:\"The plugin does not restrict which user properties a form field\'s default value can be populated from, allowing users with the Editor role and above to obtain the password hash and sensitive user meta (such as two-factor secrets) of higher-privileged users, including administrators, who view and submit the form.\";s:8:\"severity\";i:3;}i:2;O:8:\"stdClass\":7:{s:2:\"id\";s:36:\"4b2a06fc-4425-4b02-964f-f3b4a8540ba1\";s:5:\"title\";s:117:\"Contact Form 7 < 6.2.5 - Unauthenticated Non-Public Post Title, URL and Author Email Disclosure via Special Mail Tags\";s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"6.2.5\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;s:17:\"affected_versions\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"6.2.5\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;}}s:11:\"description\";s:338:\"The plugin does not check that the post a form submission says it was sent from is publicly viewable before resolving post-related mail-tags, allowing unauthenticated users to obtain the title, URL, author name and author email of private, draft and trashed posts where such tags are used in an email or message returned to the submitter.\";s:8:\"severity\";i:2;}}}s:9:\"hello.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:5:\"1.7.2\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:0:{}}s:47:\"ispmanager-auto-login/ispmanager-auto-login.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:5:\"1.2.0\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:0:{}}s:19:\"jetpack/jetpack.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:4:\"16.4\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:0:{}}s:16:\"kubio/plugin.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:5:\"2.9.4\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":7:{s:2:\"id\";s:36:\"e65debe3-17f5-4a4c-969e-d55e4094b887\";s:5:\"title\";s:132:\"Kubio AI Page Builder < 2.9.5 - Authenticated (Subscriber+) Stored Cross-Site Scripting via kubio/shortcode Block via Product Review\";s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"2.9.5\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;s:17:\"affected_versions\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:5:\"2.9.5\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;}}s:11:\"description\";s:727:\"The Kubio AI Page Builder plugin for WordPress is vulnerable to Stored Cross-Site Scripting via the \'comment (review body)\' parameter in all versions up to, and including, 2.9.4 due to insufficient input sanitization and output escaping. This makes it possible for authenticated attackers, with subscriber-level access and above, to inject arbitrary web scripts in pages that will execute whenever a user accesses an injected page. Successful exploitation requires the attacker\'s review containing the payload to be approved, either automatically (due to a previously approved review from the same account) or manually by an administrator, and the active single-product template must use a Kubio-rendered wp:post-content block.\";s:8:\"severity\";i:3;}}}s:23:\"siteleads/siteleads.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:5:\"1.2.1\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:0:{}}s:24:\"wordpress-seo/wp-seo.php\";O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:7:\"version\";s:4:\"28.7\";s:15:\"vulnerabilities\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":7:{s:2:\"id\";s:36:\"9fa2d095-5659-4af8-9815-aa6ba94377c8\";s:5:\"title\";s:93:\"Yoast SEO < 28.9 - Unauthenticated Private Content Disclosure via Headless REST Head Endpoint\";s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:4:\"28.9\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;s:17:\"affected_versions\";a:1:{i:0;O:8:\"stdClass\":2:{s:8:\"fixed_in\";s:4:\"28.9\";s:13:\"introduced_in\";N;}}s:11:\"description\";s:310:\"The plugin does not check that content is publicly viewable before returning its SEO metadata through a public REST endpoint, allowing unauthenticated users who know or can guess the URL of a private post, page or custom post type item to read its title, author, publication date and an excerpt of its content.\";s:8:\"severity\";i:2;}}}}}\";','auto'),(4266,'jetpack_protect_status_time','1795429993','auto'),(4267,'jetpack_site_products','a:0:{}','on'),(4268,'jetpack_active_plan','a:11:{s:10:\"product_id\";i:2002;s:12:\"product_slug\";s:12:\"jetpack_free\";s:12:\"product_name\";s:12:\"Jetpack Free\";s:18:\"product_name_short\";s:4:\"Free\";s:7:\"expired\";b:0;s:14:\"billing_period\";s:0:\"\";s:13:\"user_is_owner\";b:0;s:7:\"is_free\";b:1;s:11:\"license_key\